{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.9,"content_digest":"273ef77f3f27435b1feaa739564a2b533f2a2a1ca4657bac321fefa931d2bc9e","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:23+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:e6a121b5d373c64251f538b1","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:interdisciplinary-science:01M0HHZFCW9EWVR511F1QX5H7H","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim uses LSBS's EXP-completeness to infer that membership of LSBS in NP would imply EXP = NP. This is exactly the completeness-based class-collapse argument: an EXP-complete problem in NP places EXP inside NP, and since NP is contained in EXP, the classes coincide. The complexity-class scale and completeness conditions are compatible.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:23+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
